        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            The old version of this question was easy. Electric meant a corded single-stage machine that was fine on a patio and hopeless on a real driveway, so anyone with meaningful snow bought gas. That stopped being true when 56V, 60V and 80V lithium platforms arrived. Battery single-stage machines from the major brands now clear a typical two-car driveway after an average storm, and battery two-stage machines exist that handle depths only gas used to touch.
          </p>

          <p>
            What has not changed is physics. Moving snow is moving weight, and a battery pack stores far less energy than a gallon of gasoline. A gas engine can keep chewing through heavy, wet snow for as long as there is fuel in the tank, and you can refill that tank in thirty seconds. A battery machine has a fixed energy budget per charge, and dense snow spends that budget quickly. Every practical difference between the two flows from that one fact.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the useful way to frame the decision is not which type is better, but how much snow you move in a typical storm and how heavy it is. A modest driveway in a region that gets frequent light to moderate snow is squarely battery territory. A long rural driveway in the snow belt, or one that regularly sees a compacted plow berm, still points to gas. In between, a battery two-stage machine or a careful choice of single-stage model can work, and the sections below show how to tell.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Size the machine to your worst regular storm, not your average one.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If three or four storms a winter bring heavy, wet snow over 10 inches and a plow berm, the machine has to handle those days. A blower that is perfect for the other twenty snowfalls but stalls on the bad ones leaves you shoveling exactly when shoveling is hardest.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you already know electric suits you, our <a href="/best-electric-snow-blower/">ranked electric snow blower picks</a> cover the battery and corded machines worth buying. If you are leaning toward gas or not sure yet, our <a href="/best-snow-blower/">overall snow blower guide</a> includes the gas single-stage and two-stage options alongside them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="clearing-power" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Clearing Power: Snow Type, Depth and the Plow Berm
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fresh, dry powder is the easy case for any machine. It weighs little, it does not stick, and a battery single-stage blower throws it a long way with little effort. On a cold morning after six inches of light snow, the honest difference between a good battery blower and a gas one is mostly noise and the pull cord. Most owners who switch to battery and live somewhere with dry winters never look back.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wet snow is where the gap opens. Snow that falls near freezing can weigh two to three times as much per cubic foot as cold powder, and it packs into the chute and the auger housing. A gas engine has torque in reserve and simply works harder. A battery machine draws more current, which drains the pack faster and, on some models, triggers a protective slowdown. Most battery blowers still clear wet snow, but runtime can drop sharply and throw distance shrinks.
          </p>

          <p>
            The plow berm is the real test. When a municipal plow passes, it leaves a ridge of compacted, often icy snow across the end of the driveway that can be two feet high and far denser than anything that fell from the sky. Single-stage machines of either fuel type struggle with it, because the auger has to both cut and throw. Two-stage gas machines, with a slow serrated auger feeding a fast impeller, are built for exactly this job.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Depth ratings, read correctly</h3>

          <p>
            Manufacturers list a maximum clearing depth, typically around 8 to 13 inches for battery single-stage machines and 20 inches or more for two-stage designs of either fuel. Treat those figures as the intake height, not a promise. A machine can physically accept snow that deep and still need slow passes or partial-width bites to get through it. Battery two-stage models, such as the ones from EGO and Toro, narrow the gap on depth considerably, but they do it at a price close to a good gas two-stage.
          </p>

