        <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Runtime, Driveway Size and What Cold Does to Batteries
          </h2>

          <p>
            Battery runtime is usually quoted as minutes or as an area cleared per charge, and both figures assume favorable conditions. Many single-stage battery blowers are rated somewhere in the range of 30 to 50 minutes on a full set of packs. In practice runtime depends almost entirely on how heavy the snow is, because the motor draws power in proportion to the load. The same machine can finish a driveway with charge to spare in powder and run flat partway through in slush.
          </p>

          <p>
            A little arithmetic makes this concrete. A typical two-car driveway is somewhere around 600 to 800 square feet. Six inches of snow on 700 square feet is about 350 cubic feet of snow. At the low density of cold powder that is roughly a ton and a half of material, and at the density of wet snow it can be more than double that. The battery has to throw all of it, so a storm that doubles the weight roughly halves the area you can clear per charge.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why driveway size is the first filter. Up to roughly a two-car driveway plus a front walk, a battery machine with the larger packs a brand offers will usually finish on one charge in ordinary storms. Past three cars wide, or with a long single-lane approach, you either need a second set of batteries, a mid-job recharge, or a fuel tank. A spare pack set is a real solution, but it adds several hundred dollars to the purchase.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cold weather and lithium batteries</h3>

          <p>
            Lithium-ion cells deliver less power when they are cold, and most battery tool makers advise storing and charging packs indoors at room temperature. A pack that sat in an unheated garage overnight at 10 degrees Fahrenheit will start the job with less usable capacity than one carried in from the house. The fix is simple and free: keep batteries inside, put them in the machine just before you go out, and let them warm up again before recharging.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas engines have their own cold problem, which is starting. Most modern two-stage gas machines include an electric start that plugs into an outlet, and a well-maintained engine with fresh, stabilized fuel starts reliably. A machine that sat all summer with untreated fuel in the carburetor is another story, and that is the single most common reason a gas blower refuses to start on the first storm of the year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Maintenance, Noise and Storage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Maintenance is where battery machines win outright. There is no oil to change, no spark plug, no carburetor to gum up and no fuel to go stale. Ownership amounts to keeping the batteries indoors, checking the scraper bar and paddles for wear, and occasionally clearing the chute. Many owners buy battery specifically because they were tired of a gas machine that would not start in December.
          </p>

          <p>
            A gas snow blower needs an annual routine to stay reliable. Before the season that means fresh or stabilized fuel, an oil check or change, a look at the spark plug, belts and shear pins, and a test run before you need it. At the end of the season it means either running the tank dry or treating the fuel. None of it is difficult, and our guide to <a href="/how-to-prepare-a-snow-blower-for-winter/">preparing a snow blower for winter</a> walks through the whole October service, but it is a real recurring job, and skipping it is how carburetors fail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Noise is the second clear win for electric. A gas snow blower at operator position is typically loud enough that hearing protection is recommended, and early-morning clearing can be a sore point with neighbors. Battery blowers are much quieter; most of what you hear is the auger and the snow leaving the chute. If you leave for work before sunrise, that difference matters more than it sounds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Storage favors electric too. Battery single-stage machines are lighter and often fold their handles, so they hang or stand in a corner of the garage or shed. There are no fumes, so they can live in spaces where storing a fueled engine is a bad idea. Gas machines, especially two-stage models, are heavy and bulky, need a ventilated space away from ignition sources, and take a real chunk of floor space all year.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Upfront and Five-Year Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sticker prices overlap more than people expect. A battery single-stage machine with batteries and charger usually sells in roughly the same range as a comparable gas single-stage, often somewhere between about $500 and $1,000 depending on width and battery size. Battery two-stage machines generally cost well over $1,500, which puts them alongside mid-range and upper-mid-range gas two-stage machines. Corded electric throwers are the outlier, typically costing a few hundred dollars or less.
          </p>

          <p>
            The battery cost depends heavily on whether you already own the platform. If you have other tools on the same battery system, buying the bare machine and sharing packs can cut the price substantially. If you do not, the batteries can be close to half the cost of the kit, and a larger pack or a spare set adds several hundred dollars more. That shared-platform saving is one of the strongest arguments for staying with a brand you already use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running costs favor electric by a wide margin. Recharging a set of packs uses a small amount of electricity, typically well under a dollar per full charge at average residential rates. A gas machine uses fuel, stabilizer, oil, plugs and the occasional belt or shear pin, and a shop tune-up commonly costs around $100 or more if you do not do it yourself. Over a winter those costs are not large, but they add up across five years.
          </p>

          <p>
            The big unknown for battery machines is pack replacement. Lithium packs lose capacity with age and charge cycles, and a heavily used set may need replacing within the life of the machine. Replacement high-capacity packs commonly cost a few hundred dollars each. Budget for at least one replacement over a long ownership period, and the five-year totals for battery and gas end up closer than the running costs alone suggest.
          </p>

